{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1526884046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMjQ4MDE5Mjg=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2512","origin":"github"},"title":"`run` could support call placeholders for input/output","message":"I started using the input/output spec feature intensely (thanks @kyleam!) and I find myself doing variants of this pattern all the time (pretty much):\n\n```\ndatalad run -m \"Import stimulation events\" \\\n   --input inputs/rawdata/events.tsv \\\n   --output sub-02/func/sub-02_task-oneback_run-01_events.tsv \\\n   cp inputs/rawdata/events.tsv sub-02/func/sub-02_task-oneback_run-01_events.tsv\n```\n\nAdmittedly, this is the extreme case, but here and in related cases it would be nice to be able to say:\n\n```\ndatalad run -m \"Import stimulation events\" \\\n   --input inputs/rawdata/events.tsv \\\n   --output sub-02/func/sub-02_task-oneback_run-01_events.tsv \\\n   cp {input} {output}\n```\n\nsuch that `run` would transparently inject the (unexpanded) input and output specifications into the command list (multi-item extension, if there are multiple inputs/outputs, like done for `--call-fmt` in the `containers-run` command). We already support shell-style globbing, so this should work in a wide range of cases.\n\nAdditionally it could support placeholder expansion like `{input[3]}` (standard format() syntax) to reference individual items.","files":null}]}